{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-313249",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1).$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-(2x-1)^{2}}\\cdot (2x-1)^{′}=\\frac{2}{4x-4{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(4x-4{x}^{2})\\,{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{1}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
